# KTP AntiCheat data retention — uploads day-dirs (60d), weapon-timeline rows
# (30d, batched), expired session tokens (7d past expiry).
# Script: /usr/local/bin/ktp-ac-retention.sh (canonical: KTPInfrastructure/scripts/).
# 04:40 ET daily — after ktp-backup so the nightly dump still contains the day
# about to age out.
#
# ACTIVE on the data server. Was staged-disabled when first written; activating it
# there never made it back here, so this file claimed retention was off while it had
# been pruning nightly for weeks. Verified 2026-07-24 against the box: job line live
# in /etc/cron.d/ktp-ac-retention, /var/log/ktp-ac-retention.log running daily, and
# the data agrees — oldest weapon row sits exactly on the 30d horizon, oldest upload
# day-dir exactly on 60d.
#
# On a FRESH install, comment the job line out and check DRY_RUN=1 output first:
# the first run deletes everything back to the retention horizon in one go.
# UPLOAD SWEEP HELD since 2026-08-16 (operator: retain all evidence bundles).
# This line exists on the data server and did NOT exist here, so restoring this
# file from the repo removed the hold and let the script fall back to its 60d
# default. That is not hypothetical: the sweep ran nightly from 07-03 and nulled
# zip_path for 43 sessions (04-25 -> 06-15). The bundles themselves survive on the
# provider-diverse mirrors, so the cost is recovery work, not lost evidence -- but
# the API cannot reparse a session whose zip_path is NULL until both are restored.
SHELL=/bin/bash
UPLOAD_RETENTION_DAYS=36500
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
40 4 * * * root /usr/local/bin/ktp-ac-retention.sh >> /var/log/ktp-ac-retention.log 2>&1
